WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 12 defined fairness, integrity, due diligence and fiduciary responsibility and catalogued the frauds. This chapter is the professional codification of the same material: two of Chapter 12's four concepts appear here as a principle and a standard, due diligence appears inside Competence, and the fiduciary duty of confidentiality is a standard of its own.
13.1  The four principles
The Statement opens with four overarching ethical principles, which express the values members are expected to hold. They are not rules to be complied with one at a time; they are the basis on which the standards rest, and the Statement says that members shall act in accordance with these principles and shall encourage others within their organisations to do the same.
Honesty. Not deceiving: in what is said, in what is written, and in what is left out. Chapter 12 made the point that a technically true statement arranged to mislead is dishonest, and the principle covers it.
Fairness. Treating people and interests even-handedly, with the three dimensions of Chapter 12 — outcome, process and treatment.
Objectivity. Reporting and judging on the evidence, free from bias and from the influence of personal interest or of pressure from others. It is the principle that distinguishes a professional opinion from a convenient one.
Responsibility. Accepting accountability for one's actions and for one's professional obligations, including the obligation to act when something is wrong rather than to observe it.
Two points about the principles are worth having for an exam.
They apply to the individual, personally and continuously, regardless of the employer's instructions. The Statement is a commitment made by the member, and no instruction from an employer releases it.
And they are the reason the standards exist, which matters when a situation is not covered neatly by any standard. Where the rules run out, the principles decide.
