13.2  Competence
The first standard. Its obligations are four and each has teeth.
Maintain an appropriate level of professional leadership and expertise by continually developing knowledge and skills. Competence is not a state reached once; the obligation is continuing, and it extends to leadership as well as technique.
Perform professional duties in accordance with relevant laws, regulations and technical standards. This is the obligation that makes a breach of accounting standards an ethical matter and not only a technical one.
Provide decision support information and recommendations that are accurate, clear, concise and timely. All four adjectives are obligations: an accurate analysis delivered after the decision, or an accurate one nobody can follow, has not met the standard.
Recognise and help manage risk. The whole first half of this book is inside this clause, and it is the clause most candidates forget. Identifying an exposure and saying nothing is a breach of competence.
Three situations in which competence is the standard engaged.
Accepting an assignment beyond one's expertise without saying so. The obligation is not to know everything; it is to be honest about the limit and to get help, which also engages Credibility's fourth clause.
Working from a model, a spreadsheet or an assumption the professional has not understood. Chapter 7's spreadsheet error is an operational risk and also, where the preparer did not understand it, a competence failure.
And failing to keep up: applying a superseded standard, missing a change in the law, using a technique that has been replaced.
