13.4  Integrity
The third standard. Chapter 12 defined the concept; here are the Statement's four obligations.
Mitigate actual conflicts of interest, communicate regularly with business associates to avoid apparent conflicts, and advise all parties of any potential conflicts. Three categories — actual, apparent and potential — and three different duties. An apparent conflict matters even where no actual conflict exists, because the appearance damages the reliance others place on the professional's judgment.
Refrain from conduct that would prejudice carrying out duties ethically. This covers accepting gifts, hospitality or favours that could influence or appear to influence a decision, and anything that creates an obligation to somebody whose interests may diverge from the employer's.
Abstain from engaging in or supporting any activity that might discredit the profession. The word supporting is important: assisting, enabling or remaining silent about such an activity falls within it.
Contribute to a positive ethical culture and place integrity of the profession above personal interests. This is the forward-looking obligation — not merely avoiding wrong, but helping to build the culture Chapters 16 and 17 describe.
Two distinctions candidates are expected to make.
Integrity concerns the professional's own conduct and interests; Credibility concerns what is communicated to others. A conflict of interest engages Integrity; an incomplete report engages Credibility; and a report made incomplete in order to protect an interest engages both.
And a gift's value is not the only test. A modest gift from a supplier in a competitive tender is a problem where a larger one from a long-standing customer with nothing pending may not be. What matters is whether it could influence, or appear to influence, a decision.
