13.5  Credibility
The fourth standard, and the one most often engaged in exam situations, because it governs what is reported. Its obligations are four.
Communicate information fairly and objectively. Fairly covers presentation as well as content: a chart with a truncated axis, a comparison against a revised budget, a result shown before an unfavourable adjustment.
Disclose all relevant information that could reasonably be expected to influence an intended user's understanding of reports, analyses or recommendations. This is the completeness obligation, and it is the one that makes omission a breach. The test is what the user would need, not what the preparer was asked for.
Disclose delays or deficiencies in information, timeliness, processing or internal controls, in conformance with organisation policy or applicable law. A professional who knows a control has failed and says nothing has breached this clause, whether or not a loss followed.
Communicate professional limitations or other constraints that would preclude responsible judgment or successful performance of an activity. If you cannot do it properly — for lack of time, information, expertise or access — say so, to somebody who can act on it.
Three situations that engage Credibility.
A forecast prepared on assumptions the preparer believes to be unachievable, presented without that reservation.
A variance analysis that explains the favourable items and attributes the unfavourable ones to timing without evidence.
And an analysis prepared for a decision in which a known weakness — an estimate with a wide range, a figure that could not be verified, a control that was not operating — is not stated.
