LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Shall", "should" and "may"
Professional codes are written in a vocabulary of obligation, and the words are not interchangeable. Shall and must state a requirement: the member has no discretion. Should states a strong expectation that may yield to circumstances, and a member who departs from a should is expected to be able to say why. May grants permission or describes an option. English codes use all three deliberately, and a candidate reading a standard quickly will often turn a shall into a should in their memory, which changes the answer. Arabic يجب for a requirement and ينبغي for an expectation make the same distinction, as do French doit and devrait. The practical habit worth forming is this: when a situation asks whether something was permitted, find the verb in the obligation and read it exactly. Most ethics questions are decided by a verb.
