Chapter summary
Figure F813-04 brings the chapter together.
Figure F813-04. Chapter 13 summary map: the key rule for each section.
The IMA Statement of Ethical Professional Practice rests on four overarching principles — honesty, fairness, objectivity and responsibility — which bind the member personally regardless of any employer's instruction and which decide where the standards run out. Competence requires continuing development of expertise and leadership, performance in accordance with laws, regulations and technical standards, information that is accurate, clear, concise and timely, and the recognition and management of risk. Confidentiality requires information to be kept unless disclosure is authorised or legally required, those who receive it to be informed and monitored, and the information never to be used for unethical or illegal advantage — the prohibition covering use as well as disclosure, surviving the employment, and never standing in the way of reporting a concern internally. Integrity requires actual conflicts to be mitigated, apparent ones avoided and potential ones disclosed, conduct that would prejudice ethical performance refused, activities that would discredit the profession neither engaged in nor supported, and a positive ethical culture built. Credibility requires fair and objective communication, disclosure of everything that could influence an intended user's understanding, reporting of delays and deficiencies including control failures, and a statement of any limitation that would preclude responsible judgment. And applying all of it to a situation means stating the facts, naming each standard with its specific obligation, explaining why it applies to those facts, and saying where a standard is engaged without yet being breached.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
P13-01  The four overarching principles are:
A.	honesty, fairness, objectivity, responsibility.
B.	competence, confidentiality, integrity, credibility.
C.	honesty, competence, loyalty, care.
D.	fairness, diligence, loyalty, accountability.
P13-02  "Recognise and help manage risk" belongs to:
A.	Competence.
B.	Credibility.
C.	Integrity.
D.	Confidentiality.
P13-03  Information that is accurate but late breaches:
A.	Competence.
B.	Confidentiality.
C.	Integrity.
D.	no standard.
P13-04  Confidential information may be disclosed where:
A.	disclosure is authorised or legally required.
B.	it benefits the company.
C.	a colleague asks.
D.	the member has resigned.
P13-05  Using confidential information for private gain without disclosing it breaches:
A.	Confidentiality.
B.	Credibility.
C.	Competence.
D.	nothing, since nothing was disclosed.
P13-06  Advising all parties of a potential conflict of interest is required by:
A.	Integrity.
B.	Credibility.
C.	Competence.
D.	Confidentiality.
P13-07  Supporting an activity that would discredit the profession:
A.	breaches Integrity.
B.	breaches Credibility.
C.	breaches Competence.
D.	is permitted if the member does not participate.
P13-08  Disclosing all information that could influence a user's understanding is required by:
A.	Credibility.
B.	Integrity.
C.	Competence.
D.	Confidentiality.
P13-09  Failing to report a control deficiency breaches:
A.	Credibility.
B.	Confidentiality.
C.	Integrity.
D.	no standard unless a loss occurs.
P13-10  Communicating professional limitations that would preclude responsible judgment is required by:
A.	Credibility.
B.	Competence only.
C.	Integrity.
D.	Confidentiality.
P13-11  A member instructed by an employer to breach a standard:
A.	remains bound by the Statement.
B.	is released by the instruction.
C.	must comply.
D.	is bound only if a law applies.
P13-12  Naming a standard without tying it to a specific obligation and fact:
A.	is an incomplete answer.
B.	is sufficient.
C.	breaches Credibility.
D.	is the required form.
Case-style practice: the quarterly pack
Orontes's management accountant is preparing the quarterly board pack. Three things are true. The sales director has asked for the comparison to be shown against the revised forecast rather than the original budget, because the original is now unachievable, and has not asked for the change to be noted. The inventory reconciliation has not been performed for two months because the person who did it left. And a provision against a distributor's balance, agreed last quarter at 240,000, has been reduced to 90,000 on the chief executive's instruction, on the basis of a verbal assurance from the distributor that the management accountant has not seen.
Required. Identify the standards engaged, with the specific obligations, and explain why each applies. Say what the management accountant should do. Under 350 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC13-1  Answer A. Responsibility completes the four.
B is wrong. That is a standard.
C is wrong. So is that.
D is wrong. So is that.
SC13-2  Answer A. The commitment is personal and continuing.
B is wrong. The principles apply throughout.
C is wrong. Seniority is irrelevant.
D is wrong. An employer's code does not define them.
SC13-3  Answer A. The principles decide the gaps.
B is wrong. Obligations do not lapse.
C is wrong. The employer does not define ethics.
D is wrong. Law is a floor, not the standard.
SC13-4  Answer A. Risk recognition sits within Competence.
B is wrong. That is Confidentiality.
C is wrong. That is Integrity.
D is wrong. That is Credibility.
SC13-5  Answer A. Timeliness is one of the four adjectives.
B is wrong. Accuracy alone is insufficient.
C is wrong. No confidential information is involved.
D is wrong. No conflict is involved.
SC13-6  Answer A. Both standards are engaged.
B is wrong. Confidentiality is not in point.
C is wrong. Two standards are engaged.
D is wrong. Competence is engaged too.
SC13-7  Answer A. Authorised, or legally required.
B is wrong. Benefit is not an exception.
C is wrong. A request is not authority.
D is wrong. The duty survives departure.
SC13-8  Answer A. Use is prohibited as well as disclosure.
B is wrong. The prohibition covers use.
C is wrong. Competence is not engaged.
D is wrong. It is a professional breach as well.
SC13-9  Answer A. Internal reporting is not a breach.
B is wrong. The committee is entitled to know.
C is wrong. Consent from the subject is not required.
D is wrong. It is the expected route.
SC13-10  Answer A. Appearance undermines reliance.
B is wrong. It may be only apparent.
C is wrong. Legality is a separate question.
D is wrong. Competence is a different standard.
SC13-11  Answer A. Integrity is engaged regardless of value.
B is wrong. No threshold cures a tender conflict.
C is wrong. No confidential information is involved.
D is wrong. Competence is not in point.
SC13-12  Answer A. Supporting includes acquiescence.
B is wrong. Silence can be support.
C is wrong. Integrity is the standard engaged.
D is wrong. Professional obligations are additional to law.
SC13-13  Answer A. Completeness belongs to Credibility.
B is wrong. Competence concerns capability and standards.
C is wrong. Integrity concerns conflicts and conduct.
D is wrong. Confidentiality concerns disclosure.
SC13-14  Answer A. Reporting deficiencies is required.
B is wrong. The duty is not conditional on loss.
C is wrong. Confidentiality is not engaged.
D is wrong. Silence is the breach.
SC13-15  Answer A. Fair and objective communication is required.
B is wrong. No confidential matter arises.
C is wrong. Credibility is the standard engaged.
D is wrong. Accuracy does not cure misleading presentation.
SC13-16  Answer A. Standard, obligation, and application to the facts.
B is wrong. Naming alone is incomplete.
C is wrong. Law is a separate question.
D is wrong. Resignation is rarely the first step.
SC13-17  Answer A. Engagement without assertion of intent.
B is wrong. Intent must be established, not assumed.
C is wrong. Silence fails the question.
D is wrong. Standards may be engaged without proven intent.
SC13-18  Answer A. Non-disclosure is the breach.
B is wrong. The obligation is to disclose.
C is wrong. Credibility concerns communication to users.
D is wrong. No confidential information is involved.
Your turn
@table
Situation | Standard
Omitting a known write-down from a board pack | Credibility: disclose all information influencing understanding.
Buying shares on information not yet public | Confidentiality: no use for unethical or illegal advantage.
Accepting work beyond one's expertise silently | Competence, with Credibility's duty to state limitations.
Not disclosing that a supplier is a relative | Integrity: mitigate and disclose conflicts.
Not reporting a failed reconciliation control | Credibility: report deficiencies in internal controls.
Applying a superseded standard through inattention | Competence: perform duties in accordance with technical standards.
What it tells you | Six situations, four standards, and in two of them more than one standard applies — which is why an answer names each one with the obligation it rests on.
@end
